{"input_text": "Question: I was reading Richard Feynman biography when I read that one time he was able to calculate the  cube root of large number in his brain by just using simple facts of everyday life. So my question is there a way to calculate square root or cube root by using just a pen a paper, I mean a systematic way not by trial and error.  

Answer: The way I use 
to get an approximate $n$-th roots
is to use the first terms of
the binomial theorem
in the form
$$\sqrt[n]{a^n+b}
=a \sqrt[n]{1+\frac{b}{a^n}}
\sim a(1+\frac{b}{n a^n})
= a +\frac{b}{n a^{n-1}}
$$ 
 For example,
$$\sqrt[3]{130}
=\sqrt[3]{125+5}
=\sqrt[3]{5^3+5}
\sim 5+\frac{5}{3\cdot 5^2}
= 5+\frac{1}{15}
= 5.0666...
$$ 
 The correct result is $5.06579701910089...$. 
